amane","noun","shaman (male or of unspecified sex)",0,"m"]]},"schief":{"entries":[["schief",null,"/ʃiːf/","From northern Middle High German schief, schīf (etc.), from Middle Low German schêf, from Old Saxon *skêf, from Proto-West Germanic *skaib, from Proto-Germanic *skaibaz.\nRegular High German cognates exist only in north-western Central German; compare Central Franconian scheif, northernmost Luxembourgish scheef. Otherwise it was borrowed from Low German into the colonial dialects, i.e. East Central German, with fallible phonetic interpretation of the original form (hence variously -ie-, -ī-, -ē-, -ei-, -b-, -f-, etc.). The word established itself in standard German during the 18th century. Cognate with Dutch scheef, Old English *sc(e)āf (in scāffōt), Old Norse skeifr."]],"senses":[[19648,"schief","adj","skew, oblique, askew, leaning, slanting, aslant, inclined (neither parallel nor perpendicular)",0],[19649,"schief","adj","slopy, sloping (characterised by a slope or slopes)",1],[19650,"schief","adj","crooked, awry (set at an angle; not vertical or square)",2],[19651,"schief","adj","lopsided (not even or balanced; not the same on one side as on the other)",3],[19652,"schief","adj","wry (turned away, contorted)",4],[19653,"schief","adj","distorted (misshapen; brought out of shape)",5],[19654,"schief","adj","worn (damaged and shabby as a result of much use)",6],[19655,"schief","adj","inappropriate (not suitable for the situation, time, or place)",7,null,["figuratively"]],[19656,"schief","adv","crookedly, at an angle",8],[19657,"schief","adv","lopsidedly",9],[19658,"schief","adv","askance (sideways; obliquely)",10,null,["figuratively"]],[19659,"schief","adv","badly, wrong (in a way that isn't right)",11]],"related_forms":[[29467,"schief","schief ansehen","derived"],[29468,"schief","schiefe bahn","derived"],[29469,"schief","schiefe ebene","derived"],[29470,"schief","schiefgehen","derived"],[29471,"schief","schieflaufen","derived"]]},"schikanöse":{"entries":[["schika